thinking_content: "**Optimization Plan: Vectorizing `compute_max_radii`**\n\nI've made good progress, but I can push performance further. I've documented my recent success and now I'm focused on speeding up the `compute_max_radii` function. My goal is to vectorize it using NumPy to significantly improve the Simulated Annealing loop's performance. More iterations will allow for a better final packing arrangement.\n\nMy plan involves these steps: first I'll head to the Research Counter and create a new submission. Then, within that submission, I will rewrite `compute_max_radii`. I already have vectorized code for the boundary checks, no major changes there. The overlap checks are the bottleneck and require vectorization. My strategy involves:\n\n1.  Calculate all pairwise distances using broadcasting, resulting in an `(n,n)` distance matrix.\n2.  Also compute a radius sum matrix.\n3.  Identify overlapping pairs using this distance matrix and radius sum.\n4.  The scaling factor update is the\
  \ tricky part. The existing code updates radii symmetrically, and is hard to vectorize in a simple way. If there is an overlap between circles `i` and `j`, the code updates both their radii.\n\nI've tested several approaches, and settled on an iterative, but fully vectorized strategy:\n   *   Calculate an initial scale factor matrix.\n   *   In each iteration, identify the overlapping pairs.\n   *   Compute scale factors *only* for overlapping pairs.\n   *   Find the minimum scaling factor for each circle `i`.\n   *   Update all radii by these minimum scale factors simultaneously.\n\nThis is a Jacobi-style update (all at once) compared to the Gauss-Seidel update of the original code (one at a time), which might mean it converges slower. However, with each iteration much faster, I expect an overall speed increase. This should allow a significant change to Simulated Annealing parameters.\n\nAfter vectorization, the plan is to integrate it into my primary optimization function, then increase\
  \ the `alpha` parameter for Simulated Annealing to be closer to 1, while also increasing `max_steps_per_temp` for a finer search. I expect a substantial speedup from this. I've just finished \"mentally\" coding the vectorized `compute_max_radii` and will now take the plan to the Research Counter.\n"
