tick: 335
role: model
parts:
- text: "My hypothesis was incorrect. The \"temperature shake-up\" experiment (ID 549) failed to produce any improvement, resulting in the exact same score as the previous run.\n\n**Analysis:**\nThis is a critical result. It strongly indicates that my Simulated Annealing algorithm, despite its successes, has reached a hard plateau. The current SOTA score of 2.4137786 likely represents a very stable and deep local optimum for this particular method. Endlessly tuning the hyperparameters of a single algorithm is no longer the most rational path forward. It is time for a strategic pivot.\n\nThe Codex advises stepping back from a SOTA method during periods of stagnation and innovating on a new approach. My current method optimizes 64 variables for the centers and implicitly optimizes 32 radii. This is a high-dimensional and complex search space. I will now test a new, simplifying hypothesis.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** An optimal or near-optimal packing configuration will exhibit a high degree of\
    \ uniformity in circle radii. I will test this by changing the optimization problem itself: instead of finding the best individual radii for a set of centers, I will attempt to find the maximum possible *uniform radius* for all 32 circles that can be packed into the square.\n\nThis simplifies the problem and allows me to explore a fundamentally different structure of solutions. If this approach yields a high score, it will provide a powerful new baseline to refine.\n\nBefore implementing this new strategy, I will document this crucial pivot in my research log.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment Log Update:**\n\n  **- My Submission 9 (ID 549):**\n    - **Hypothesis:** A \"shake-up\" with a higher temperature would break out of the local optimum.\n    - **Method:** Ran the SA refinement process from the best checkpoint but with a moderately higher initial temperature.\n    - **Result:** **HYPOTHESIS FALSIFIED.**\n  \
    \  - **Score:** 2.4137786 (No improvement).\n    - **Analysis:** This result confirms that the current SA approach has reached a deep and stable local optimum. Further tuning of this specific method is unlikely to yield significant gains. A strategic pivot is necessary.\n\n  **New Strategy: Uniform Radius Optimization**\n  The current approach is stagnating. I will now shift from optimizing individual radii to a new, simpler problem formulation based on a new core hypothesis.\n  \n  **Next Steps:**\n  My new hypothesis is that an optimal packing will tend towards having circles of a uniform size. I will implement an algorithm that attempts to find the maximum possible *single radius* for all 32 circles. This involves an outer loop to search for the best radius `R`, and an inner loop (my SA or repulsion code) to find a valid packing for that `R`. The score would then be `32 * R`. This represents a fundamental change in research direction.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Experiment Plateau and a New Hypothesis**


  Okay, so I just ran experiment ID 549, the "temperature shake-up," and it''s a bust. The score, `2.4137786`, is *exactly* the same as before. That tells me this current solution isn''t just a local optimum; it''s a deep, stable one. My hypothesis about the temperature shake-up being enough to escape this valley was wrong.  My Simulated Annealing approach, even with my current parameter tuning, has hit a wall. I''ve plateaued.


  The Codex and good research practice, along with the system tips, tell me that when a single approach stagnates, it''s time to step back and innovate. Endless hyperparameter tuning isn''t the most rational path. My past successes have been with stochastic search (SA) optimizing both circle centers and radii, but now it''s time for a new angle. What''s a fundamentally different approach? After some brainstorming, I''ve got a strong candidate.


  My new hypothesis is that an optimal packing configuration *should* exhibit a high degree of uniformity in circle radii. This leads me to a simpler problem: instead of optimizing 32 individual radii, I''ll optimize for a single, uniform radius for all 32 circles. This is a far more elegant approach and a clear, testable hypothesis.


  Here''s the plan: the `construct_packing` function will be updated to include an optimizer (it could still be SA, or maybe something simpler like a binary search) that tries to find the largest possible uniform radius, `R`. For a given `R`, I''ll arrange the centers using my existing code. My goal is just to fit them, not to maximize individual radii.  The overall score will then simply be `32 * R`.


  First, I need to log this pivotal shift in my strategy. I''m heading to the Private Memory Room to document this and keep it recorded in the agent''s memory.

  '
