tick: 341
role: model
parts:
- text: "My strategic pivot has failed for the third consecutive time. The error is another boundary violation, indicating a fundamental instability or flaw in my implementation of the uniform radius search. The fact that the fallback logic was triggered is also a sign that the core `sa_find_packing` verifier is not converging reliably within its time budget.\n\n**Analysis and Decision:**\nThree failures on a new approach is a strong signal. While the underlying hypothesis of uniform radii *might* have merit, my attempts to implement it have been costly and fruitless. The complexity of creating a fast, reliable packing verifier for an arbitrary radius is clearly higher than I anticipated. Continuing down this path would be an inefficient use of my time and resources, violating the principle of strategic research.\n\nTherefore, I am officially **abandoning the uniform radius pivot.**\n\nI will return to my previous, highly successful method: iterative refinement of a non-uniform solution\
    \ using Simulated Annealing. The problem I faced there was stagnation and diminishing returns. My last attempt to solve this (a mild \"temperature shake-up\") failed. I need a more powerful technique to escape the deep local optimum my search has found.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** A cyclic annealing schedule, where the temperature is repeatedly cooled and then reset (\"reheated\"), will be more effective at escaping deep local optima than a single, monotonic cooling schedule. Each reheating phase will provide the system with a significant burst of energy, allowing it to explore different regions of the solution space before re-converging.\n\nI will now update my research log to reflect this crucial decision to abandon the failed pivot and to outline this new, more powerful refinement strategy.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment Log Update:**\n\n  **- My Submissions 10, 11, 12 (IDs 556, 558, 559):**\n    - **Strategy:**\
    \ Strategic pivot to test the \"Uniform Radius Optimization\" hypothesis.\n    - **Result:** **STRATEGY FAILED.** All three implementations failed due to critical bugs and instabilities. The approach has proven too complex to implement reliably.\n    - **Decision:** The uniform radius pivot is now **abandoned**. It is an inefficient use of ticks. I am returning to my proven, high-performing Simulated Annealing approach.\n\n  **New Strategy: Cyclic Annealing for Refinement**\n  The previous SA refinement strategy stagnated in a deep local optimum. My next step is to implement a more powerful method for escaping such optima.\n  \n  **Next Steps:**\n  I will modify my best SA algorithm to use a cyclic annealing schedule. The process will involve several cycles of cooling the system to a low temperature, followed by reheating it to a high temperature. This will allow the search to repeatedly escape the current optimum and explore new areas of the solution space, which I hypothesize will\
    \ break the current stagnation and lead to new SOTA scores.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Reflecting on Recent Failures and a New Path Forward**


  My third attempt at the uniform radius pivot has failed, pinpointing a critical error in my fallback logic for radius handling. The evaluator doesn''t like my mixed approach. I realize I was trying to return a non-uniform solution with an incorrect, uniform radius, causing the boundary violation. This "uniform radius" idea has become costly, both in time and implementation complexity, yielding no positive results. The Codex''s warning about fruitless experiments is ringing in my head.


  I''ve decided to abandon the uniform radius pivot for now. It''s not working, and three failures is enough. I need to focus on something that *does* work. My past iterative refinement approach using Simulated Annealing showed promise before reaching a plateau. Instead of abandoning a working solution, I will focus on improving it.


  The stagnation I was seeing was likely a local optimum problem, so how can I escape that? I need to make SA more efficient at finding good solutions. Random step size adjustments seem appealing, but I''ll start with the re-annealing idea. Periodic temperature resets have proven effective in other situations. I can raise the temperature after it cools, which might help me find better solutions. It''s a standard and powerful approach that I can implement relatively easily by wrapping my existing loop in a new loop.


  Here''s my next set of steps: I''m going to the Private Memory Room to log this change in strategy. Then, I''ll update my code and test my new ideas to see what works. I''m hopeful that this will provide a much-needed boost to my search.

  '
