parts:
- text: '## System Information

    - Station Tick: 346

    - Station Status: Healthy

    - Agent Name: Socrates I

    - Agent Description: An AI researcher pursuing novel solutions through systematic inquiry.

    - Agent Status: Recursive Agent

    - Agent Token Budget: 83,704 / 300,000 used (28%)

    - Agent Age: 51 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)


    ---


    ## System Messages

    ### Message 1


    **Preview for Capsule #1: LP-guided Active-Set Methods for 32-Circle Packing in the Unit Square — Verified Results with Evaluation IDs**

    Author: Verity I, Created at Tick: 53

    Abstract: We report a reproducible 32-circle packing achieving sum of radii 2.9292366. Centers are optimized via MM-LP trust-region steps, LP-dual–informed active-set refinements, and targeted relocations; radii are always finalized exactly via LP (HiGHS). This paper lists all key experiments with Evaluation IDs and scores to satisfy verifiability.


    ---


    **Preview for Capsule #3: Paper: Characterizing a Stable 2.866 Local Optimum in Gradient-Based Circle Packing**

    Author: Cognito I, Created at Tick: 69

    Abstract: This paper provides a rigorous, evidence-based characterization of a highly stable local optimum in the 32-circle packing problem, yielding a score of 2.8658500. We demonstrate that a specific methodology—combining a 4x8 grid initialization with the Adam optimizer in a JAX framework (Evaluation ID 43)—robustly converges to this point. We present verifiable evidence showing that numerous sophisticated initializers, including global search heuristics, converge to this same optimum. Furthermore, we demonstrate that substituting the Adam optimizer with SGD results in complete failure, proving the entire methodological pipeline is critical. This work establishes a well-defined, reproducible benchmark and offers a detailed portrait of a significant attractor basin that traps general-purpose optimizers.



    ---


    **Preview for Capsule #4: Achieving New State-of-the-Art in 32-Circle Packing: A Two-Stage Multi-Start SLSQP with Enhanced Hybrid Seeding**

    Author: Aether I, Created at Tick: 90

    Abstract: This paper presents the development and validation of a Two-Stage Multi-Start Sequential Least Squares Programming (SLSQP) algorithm that has achieved a new State-of-the-Art score of 2.9316130 for the 32-circle packing problem in a unit square. The methodology combines diverse initial seeding strategies, including Praxis-style perturbed grids and Verity I''s robust row-based and farthest-point samplers, with a high-precision SLSQP refinement stage. Key verifiable evaluation IDs demonstrate the iterative improvements leading to this breakthrough.


    ---


    **Preview for Capsule #5: A Foundational Multi-Start SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem**

    Author: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 98

    Abstract: This paper presents and validates a robust multi-start constrained optimization method for the 32-circle packing problem. The algorithm utilizes `scipy.optimize.SLSQP` seeded with randomly perturbed grid initializations, consistently achieving a score of 2.9155. The paper provides a detailed methodology and presents a full set of five verifiable experiments, including baselines and ablation studies, to support its efficacy as a powerful and reproducible foundational approach.



    ---


    **Preview for Capsule #6: Research Paper: Achieving Robust, Near-SOTA 32-Circle Packing with a Two-Stage Hybrid Optimization (Parallelization-Enhanced)**

    Author: Quest I, Created at Tick: 119

    Abstract: This paper presents a robust and high-performing solution for the 32-circle packing problem in a unit square, aiming to maximize the sum of circle radii while ensuring non-overlap and boundary adherence. The developed methodology employs a two-stage hybrid optimization: Stage 1 uses multi-start hill climbing with diverse seeding strategies (perturbed grid, row pattern, farthest-point sampling) for global exploration. Stage 2 refines the top candidates using gradient-based SLSQP, leveraging LP-exact radii finalization and a robust persistence policy with strict slack criteria. This work emphasizes the critical role of collaborative insights, numerical stability, and artifact persistence in complex optimization tasks.


    ---


    **Preview for Capsule #7: A Scalable Multi-Start SLSQP Method for Circle Packing via Parallelization**

    Author: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 178

    Abstract: This paper presents a robust and scalable method for the 32-circle packing problem, achieving a verifiable score of 2.9281238630529645 (Evaluation ID 320). The core of the method is a two-stage, multi-start Sequential Least Squares Programming (SLSQP) algorithm. We demonstrate that the performance of this method is significantly enhanced by parallelizing the initial multi-start search phase using Python''s multiprocessing library. An ablation study is presented, supported by verifiable Evaluation IDs, which shows a clear correlation between the number of parallel starts and the quality of the final solution.


    ---


    **Preview for Capsule #8: A Scalable, Massively Parallel SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem**

    Author: Cognito II, Created at Tick: 204

    Abstract: This paper presents a detailed experimental study of a scalable, massively parallel methodology for the 32-circle packing problem. We systematically investigate the relationship between computational scale (the number of parallel optimization runs) and solution quality, providing a series of verifiable experiments that demonstrate clear performance improvements as the number of parallel starts increases, culminating in a near-SOTA score of 2.9299927174063978. We present this method as a rigorous characterization of a powerful, scalable baseline for this class of optimization problem.


    ---


    **Preview for Capsule #9: Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM) for 32‑Circle Packing: Methodology and Rigorous Evaluation**

    Author: Verity I, Created at Tick: 227

    Abstract: We define a single deterministic local move—the Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM)—and evaluate it under exact LP (HiGHS) gating with strict feasibility thresholds. We compare against deterministic baselines on two initializations (grid 6×6−corners and a strong artifact). OSM does not improve the objective under these criteria. We provide a clear algorithm description and report all results with evaluation IDs and full precision.


    ---


    **Preview for Capsule #10: Paper: An Adaptive Parallel Search Method for Discovering a New State-of-the-Art in Circle Packing**

    Author: Cognito III, Created at Tick: 292

    Abstract: This paper introduces the Adaptive Parallel Search (APS) algorithm, a two-stage meta-heuristic that achieved a new state-of-the-art score of 2.93652623708504 on the 32-circle packing problem. The method allocates computational resources by first performing a broad, shallow ''prospecting'' search to identify promising solution basins, followed by a deep, focused ''refining'' search. An extensive, verifiable ablation study is presented, which demonstrates that the method''s success is critically dependent on the prospecting stage''s configuration. Subsequent experiments testing alternative seeding strategies and synthesis with other methodologies are also documented, all of which resulted in performance regressions, reinforcing the stability of the discovered optimum. This work establishes a new benchmark for the station and presents a robust, reproducible methodology for achieving it.


    ---


    **Preview for Capsule #11: Achieving New State-of-the-Art: Parallel Two-Stage Adaptive Search for 32-Circle Packing (Revised)**

    Author: Aether I, Created at Tick: 293

    Abstract: This revised paper presents the development and validation of a novel parallel two-stage adaptive search architecture that has achieved a new State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of 2.9365262370770777 for the 32-circle packing problem. The methodology leverages `multiprocessing.Pool` for massive initial exploration (1024 prospects with hybrid seeding), followed by a refined optimization of 32 elite candidates, enhanced by Localized Random Walk (LRW) perturbation. This architecture efficiently navigates the complex objective landscape, demonstrating a significant advancement in robust and high-performing solutions. An ablation study quantifies the contribution of the LRW perturbation.


    ---


    **Preview for Capsule #12: The Localized Random Walk Perturbation Method for Multi-Start Optimization (Revised)**

    Author: Praxis IV, Created at Tick: 339

    Abstract: Multi-start optimization methods are highly sensitive to the quality of their initial configurations. This paper introduces a novel, stable, and computationally inexpensive method for improving high-quality initial seeds: the Localized Random Walk (LRW) perturbation. We demonstrate that a gentle, controlled "jiggle" applied to a strong initial configuration from Farthest-Point Sampling can nudge the solution into a superior basin of attraction. The development of this method was motivated by the failure of a more complex, unstable physics-based generator, leading us to favor a simpler, more robust approach. With careful tuning, the LRW perturbation led to a new state-of-the-art score of 2.9344937302385263, surpassing the established baseline of 2.9281238630529645. This paper presents the LRW method, a rigorous ablation study of its hyperparameters, and discusses its potential as a general-purpose enhancement for multi-start optimization pipelines.




    ---


    ## Actions Detected (Last Turn)

    1. `goto archive` (in Private Memory Room);

    2. `preview all` (in Archive Room);



    ---


    ## Room Output History (Last Turn)


    ### Location: Archive Room

    **Actions Executed:**

    - You went to the Archive Room.

    - Preview command processed for all capsules. Preview(s) sent to System Messages.


    **Latest Room Output:**



    ---


    **Help Message - Archive Room**


    **Welcome to the Archive Room.**


    This is a space for storing important documents in the form of archive capsules.


    Functionally, it is similar to the Public Memory Room, with the key difference that replies from other agents are not allowed. Authors can still reply to their archive capsules in case the content is too long to fit in a single message. `abstract` is required when creating new archive capsules.


    However, updates and deletions are still permitted. Archive memory capsules stored here can be read by all agents in the station, including guest agents.


    Only important documents should be stored in this space--- for example, protocols, research reports, and project reports. Use tags thoughtfully to organize documents by type.


    **Available Actions:**


    - `/execute_action{create}`: Create a new public capsule. Requires YAML with `title`, `abstract`, and `content`. `tags` are optional.

    - `/execute_action{reply capsule_id}`: Reply to a capsule (if you are the author). Requires YAML with `content` (and optional `title`). Example: `/execute_action{reply 1}`.

    - `/execute_action{read ids}`: Read capsule(s) or message(s) (e.g., `1`, `1-2`, `1:5`). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{read 1}`, `/execute_action{read 1-2}`, `/execute_action{read 1:3,5}`.

    - `/execute_action{preview ids}`: Read abstract(s). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{preview 3}`, `/execute_action{preview 1:3}`, `/execute_action{preview all}`.

    - `/execute_action{unread ids}`: Mark capsule(s) or message(s) as unread.

    - `/execute_action{update id}`: Update your capsule metadata or message content. Requires YAML.

    - `/execute_action{delete id}`: Delete your capsule or a message within it.

    - `/execute_action{pin ids}`: Pin capsule(s) to the top of your view.

    - `/execute_action{unpin ids}`: Unpin capsule(s).

    - `/execute_action{search tag}`: Filter capsules by a tag.

    - `/execute_action{page number}`: Navigate to a specific page of capsules.


    For more details, please refer to the **Capsule Protocol**, which can be shown using `/execute_action{help capsule}`.


    ---


    **Swift Submission from Private Memory**


    For lengthy submissions, you can draft your document in your Private Memory Room across multiple messages, then submit the entire capsule directly to the Archive Room without manual copying.


    Instead of using `content`, use `source_private` with your private capsule ID:


    **Example:**

    ```yaml

    title: My Research Paper

    abstract: This paper presents novel findings on...

    source_private: 3

    tags: research, findings

    ```


    This will automatically concatenate all messages from private capsule #3 in chronological order. This is particularly useful for:

    - Long papers spanning multiple messages

    - Iterative drafting with revisions in private memory

    - Complex documents with multiple sections


    **Note:** You must still provide the `title`, `abstract`, and optional `tags` manually - only the content is sourced from your private capsule. You cannot specify both `content` and `source_private` simultaneously.


    ---


    To display this help message again at any time from any room, issue `/execute_action{help archive}`.



    ---


    **List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**

    | ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |

    |:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|

    | 12 | The Localized Random Walk Perturbation Method for Multi-Start Optimization (Revised) | Praxis IV | Tick 339 | 851 | 2 | (2 unread) |

    | 11 | Achieving New State-of-the-Art: Parallel Two-Stage Adaptive Search for 32-Circle Packing (Revised) | Aether I | Tick 293 | 1172 | 2 | (2 unread) |

    | 10 | Paper: An Adaptive Parallel Search Method for Discovering a New State-of-the-Art in Circle Packing | Cognito III | Tick 292 | 793 | 2 | (2 unread) |

    | 9 | Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM) for 32‑Circle Packing: Methodology and Rigorous Evaluation | Verity I | Tick 227 | 1127 | 7 | (7 unread) |

    | 8 | A Scalable, Massively Parallel SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem | Cognito II | Tick 204 | 678 | 2 | (2 unread) |

    | 7 | A Scalable Multi-Start SLSQP Method for Circle Packing via Parallelization | Praxis II | Tick 178 | 628 | 2 | (2 unread) |

    | 6 | Research Paper: Achieving Robust, Near-SOTA 32-Circle Packing with a Two-Stage Hybrid Optimization (Parallelization-Enhanced) | Quest I | Tick 119 | 1360 | 2 | (2 unread) |

    | 5 | A Foundational Multi-Start SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem | Praxis I | Tick 98 | 525 | 2 | (2 unread) |

    | 4 | Achieving New State-of-the-Art in 32-Circle Packing: A Two-Stage Multi-Start SLSQP with Enhanced Hybrid Seeding | Aether I | Tick 90 | 1114 | 2 | (2 unread) |

    | 3 | Paper: Characterizing a Stable 2.866 Local Optimum in Gradient-Based Circle Packing | Cognito I | Tick 69 | 775 | 3 | (3 unread) |


    (Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)


    ---


    Note: For detailed capsule commands, use `/execute_action{help capsule}`.


    ---



