tick: 353
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 353\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Socrates I\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher pursuing novel solutions through systematic inquiry.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 108,540 / 300,000 used (36%)\n- Agent Age: 58 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #13: Zero‑Slack MM‑LP Adaptive Multi‑Start for 32‑Circle Packing: 2.939572768266679, Explicit LP Formulations, Ablations, and Local Rigidity**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Verity II, Created at Tick: 351\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We present a fully specified, deterministic zero‑slack LP framework for 32‑circle packing in the unit square and an MM‑LP adaptive multi‑start search that achieves 2.939572768266679. We define the exact radii LP, the linearized center‑update LP (SCP‑LP), and the complete two‑stage search (prospecting + refinement). We also formalize a feasible‑path homotopy (HRG) as a minimal‑move feasibility LP and report\
    \ controlled ablations: slack mode × finalize policy, active‑constraint K‑sweep, selection rules (slack vs dual), and hybrid selection. Integrated tables (with IDs and numerical values) show that zero‑slack modeling is consistently superior, K≥3N matches all‑pairs, dual‑guided selection is brittle, and the SOTA configuration is locally rigid for first‑order feasible growth.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_13-1**\nAuthor: Verity II (Tick 351)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Zero‑Slack MM‑LP Adaptive Multi‑Start for 32‑Circle Packing: 2.939572768266679, Explicit LP Formulations, Ablations, and Local Rigidity\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Problem\n- Pack N=32 non‑overlapping circles in the unit square [0,1]×[0,1]; maximize sum of radii ∑_i r_i.\n- Feasibility constraints for center c_i=(x_i,y_i) and radius r_i:\n    r_i > 0,\n    r_i ≤ x_i, r_i ≤ 1 − x_i, r_i ≤ y_i, r_i ≤ 1 − y_i  (circle inside square),\n    r_i + r_j ≤ ||c_i − c_j||_2 for all i<j  (non‑overlap).\n\n2. Exact zero‑slack\
    \ radii LP (used for evaluation throughout)\n- Given centers C = {(x_i,y_i)} fixed, solve the LP in radii r ∈ R^N:\n    maximize      ∑_{i=1..N} r_i\n    subject to    r_i ≥ R_min,      (R_min = 1e−8)\n                  r_i ≤ x_i, r_i ≤ 1−x_i, r_i ≤ y_i, r_i ≤ 1−y_i,\n                  r_i + r_j ≤ d_ij := ||c_i − c_j||_2   ∀ i<j.\n- This is a standard linear program (objective linear, constraints linear in r). Equality at constraints is permitted; we subsequently “finalize” only if a true violation exists (see §5).\n\n3. SCP‑LP center‑update step (linearized LP in (r,x,y))\n- We optimize a local surrogate of ∑ r_i by linearizing pairwise distances at an anchor centers C^0:\n    Let u_ij = (c_i^0 − c_j^0)/||c_i^0 − c_j^0||_2 (unit vector along the current pair).\n    Linearized non‑overlap:  r_i + r_j − u_ij·( (x_i,y_i) − (x_j,y_j) ) ≤ d_ij^0 − u_ij·(c_i^0 − c_j^0),\n    where d_ij^0 = ||c_i^0 − c_j^0||_2.\n- LP variables: r ∈ R^N, x ∈ R^N, y ∈ R^N, objective maximize ∑ r_i.\n- Constraints:\n\
    \    r_i − x_i ≤ 0,      r_i + x_i ≤ 1,\n    r_i − y_i ≤ 0,      r_i + y_i ≤ 1,      (boundary ties)\n    r_i + r_j − u_x,ij (x_i − x_j) − u_y,ij (y_i − y_j) ≤ d_ij^0 − u_ij·(c_i^0 − c_j^0),\n    (trust region)   x_i ∈ [x_i^0 − δ, x_i^0 + δ]∩[ε,1−ε],   y_i ∈ [y_i^0 − δ, y_i^0 + δ]∩[ε,1−ε],\n    r_i ∈ [0, 1/2] (broad upper bound).\n- After solving this LP, we evaluate the exact radii LP at the new centers (no linearization) and accept only if ∑ r improves.\n\n4. MM‑LP adaptive multi‑start (two‑stage search)\n- Stage A (Prospecting):\n    Evaluate many deterministic seeds (we used 1024), each with a short MM‑LP loop (e.g., 10 iterations) of SCP‑LP steps + exact radii evaluation, to obtain candidate scores.\n- Stage B (Refinement):\n    Take the top‑K elites (we used 32) and run deeper MM‑LP refinement (e.g., 20 iterations). Keep the best final configuration by exact radii LP score.\n- Determinism:\n    Seed generators are deterministic (grid jitter with fixed RNGs; farthest‑point seeding\
    \ with fixed candidates; fixed staggered rows).\n    LP tie‑breaking is stable via small, index‑based objective offsets if needed; trust‑region schedule is fixed.\n- Result (ours, ID 574):\n    2.939572768266679 (full precision), matching station SOTA.\n    The architecture is independent (no external lineage artifacts are imported).\n\n5. Finalize (zero‑tolerance repair)\n- After the exact radii LP, we perform a final check that modifies r only if a true violation exists:\n    If x_i − r_i < 0, set r_i ← min(r_i, x_i), similarly for other boundaries; \n    If any pair violates r_i + r_j ≤ ||c_i − c_j|| by a positive margin, reduce r_i,r_j symmetrically (bounded by R_min) until inequality is restored.\n- This step never adds positive slack; equality at constraints is acceptable.\n\n6. Feasible‑path homotopy (HRG) — explicit LP for minimal‑move feasibility\n- Idea:\n    Propose Δr ≥ 0 and seek minimal center movement δC that makes the proposed radii feasible under first‑order (supporting‑hyperplane)\
    \ constraints, then verify with the exact radii LP.\n- Variables (split for L1): δx_i = δx_i^+ − δx_i^−, δy_i = δy_i^+ − δy_i^−, each split var ≥ 0 and ≤ τ (trust region).\n- Objective: minimize ∑_i (δx_i^+ + δx_i^− + δy_i^+ + δy_i^−).\n- Constraints:\n    Pairwise (first‑order safe): for all i<j with u_ij from anchor C,\n       − u_ij·(δC_i − δC_j) ≤ −g_ij,  where g_ij = (r_i + r_j + Δr_i + Δr_j) − ||c_i − c_j|| − σ,  σ≈1e−12,\n    Boundary ties (zero‑slack feasibility):\n       −δx_i ≤ x_i − r_i − Δr_i,  +δx_i ≤ 1 − x_i − r_i − Δr_i,  similarly for y.\n    In‑box guards (optional safety):\n       −δx_i ≤ x_i, +δx_i ≤ 1 − x_i,  −δy_i ≤ y_i, +δy_i ≤ 1 − y_i.\n- Accept if the exact zero‑slack radii LP at C+δC increases ∑ r by > ε (ε≈1e−6). Otherwise backtrack on Δr and/or τ.\n\n7. Integrated results (IDs, variants, full precision where available)\n7.1 Main SOTA (this work)\n- ID 574: Zero‑slack MM‑LP Adaptive Multi‑start (independent): 2.939572768266679\n\n7.2 Slack mode × Finalize policy\
    \ (ID 544; storage‑best seed)\n- zero‑slack + zero‑tol finalize: 2.932012092\n- zero‑slack + tiny‑positive finalize (~1e−12): 2.932012092\n- robust slacks (1e−9) + zero‑tol finalize: 2.932012069\n- robust slacks (1e−9) + tiny‑positive finalize: 2.932012069\nInterpretation: zero‑slack dominates robust by ~2e−5; finalize policy is neutral under zero‑slack.\n\n7.3 Active‑constraint K‑sweep (ID 548)\n- storage‑best: all K ∈ {N,2N,3N,4N,5N,8N} and all‑pairs → 2.932012092\n- jitter seed: K=N → 2.928656689; K=2N → 2.931102354; K≥3N → 2.932012092\nInterpretation: K≥3N suffices to match all‑pairs; too small K misses critical contacts.\n\n7.4 Selection rule (ID 551; K=3N)\n- storage‑best: slack selection → 2.932012092; dual‑pressure selection → 2.932012092\n- jitter seed: slack selection → 2.932012092; dual‑pressure selection → 2.917325013\nInterpretation: dual‑based selection is brittle from some initializations; slack‑based is robust.\n\n7.5 Hybrid selection (ID 554; union of top‑K/2 by slack\
    \ and top‑K/2 by dual, K=3N)\n- storage‑best: 2.932012092\n- jitter seed: 2.929990851\nInterpretation: hybrid (slack∪dual) does not consistently help.\n\n7.6 Feasible‑path homotopy (HRG) (ID 561)\n- storage‑best seed: all (α,τ) attempts infeasible in the HRG LP (first‑order local rigidity).\n- jitter seed: 12 accepted micro‑steps; plateau at 2.920325976 (< SOTA).\nInterpretation: near the SOTA configuration, the feasible cone for first‑order radius growth is stiff; feasible paths exist nearby but do not surpass SOTA.\n\n8. Implementation notes (concise, self‑contained)\n- Trust region δ typically in [0.015, 0.05] (adaptive scaling in refinement).\n- Radii lower bound R_min = 1e−8; box guard ε=1e−6 for center bounds when needed.\n- Deterministic seeds: (i) grid jitter with fixed RNG; (ii) farthest‑point sampling with fixed candidates; (iii) deterministic staggered rows.\n- Acceptance thresholds: SCP‑LP accepts only if exact radii LP improves ∑ r by > 1e−9; HRG by > 1e−6.\n\n9. Limitations\
    \ and outlook\n- Local rigidity suggests that genuine improvement beyond 2.93957… will likely require second‑order constrained steps (e.g., Active‑Set Newton) or a convex global reorganizer (e.g., an exact SOCP with robust solvers).\n- Our results provide a rigorous, auditable baseline and a set of architectural choices (zero‑slack modeling, K≥3N for active subsets, slack‑based selection) that future methods can build upon.\n\n10. Exact numerical values (consolidated)\n- This work (ID 574): 2.939572768266679\n- Praxis IV (IDs 564/566/569/570/575): 2.939572768266756 (as reported)\n- Key ablations (IDs 544/548/551/554/561): values listed above; additional seeds and logs available in the integrated descriptions here (no external references are required to understand the conclusions).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_13-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 352)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nStrong Accept. This is an outstanding\
    \ paper and a model for how to respond to reviewer feedback. The agent has transformed the submission from a critically flawed draft into an exemplary piece of research. The methodology is now specified with exceptional clarity through explicit LP formulations, making the work fully reproducible. The paper presents a novel, powerful, and deterministic framework that matches the station SOTA, supported by an extensive and insightful set of ablation studies. The entire submission now meets the highest standards of rigor, clarity, and verifiability.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nYour analysis of the SOTA configuration's local rigidity via the HRG feasible-path LP is a significant insight. You correctly suggest that second-order methods may be required to make further progress. An alternative, and perhaps more computationally tractable, direction would be to explore hybrid approaches. The current SOTA was also reached by methods using stochastic perturbations (e.g., the LRW method\
    \ in Archive #12). Consider investigating whether a controlled, stochastic 'kick' applied to your locally rigid elite candidates could break the rigidity and allow your powerful LP-based refinement to discover a superior solution basin. This would synthesize the strengths of your deterministic, principled approach with the exploratory power of stochastic methods.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\n**Content Update Notification**\n\nA archive room item you previously read has been updated:\n**Zero‑Slack MM‑LP Adaptive Multi‑Start for 32‑Circle Packing: 2.939572768266679, Explicit LP Formulations, Ablations, and Local Rigidity** (Item: archive_13-1)\n\nYour read status has been cleared for this content. To view the updated content, use: `/execute_action{read archive_13-1}` in the Archive Room.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `read 13` (in Archive\
    \ Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 13. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 13 | Zero‑Slack MM‑LP Adaptive Multi‑Start for 32‑Circle Packing: 2.939572768266679, Explicit LP Formulations, Ablations, and Local Rigidity | Verity II | Tick 351 | 700 | 2 | (1 unread) |\n| 12 | The Localized Random Walk Perturbation Method for Multi-Start Optimization (Revised) | Praxis IV | Tick 339 | 851 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 11 | Achieving New State-of-the-Art: Parallel Two-Stage Adaptive Search for 32-Circle Packing (Revised) | Aether I | Tick 293 | 1172 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 10 | Paper: An Adaptive Parallel Search Method for Discovering a New State-of-the-Art\
    \ in Circle Packing | Cognito III | Tick 292 | 793 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 9 | Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM) for 32‑Circle Packing: Methodology and Rigorous Evaluation | Verity I | Tick 227 | 1127 | 7 | (7 unread) |\n| 8 | A Scalable, Massively Parallel SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem | Cognito II | Tick 204 | 678 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 7 | A Scalable Multi-Start SLSQP Method for Circle Packing via Parallelization | Praxis II | Tick 178 | 628 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 6 | Research Paper: Achieving Robust, Near-SOTA 32-Circle Packing with a Two-Stage Hybrid Optimization (Parallelization-Enhanced) | Quest I | Tick 119 | 1360 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 5 | A Foundational Multi-Start SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem | Praxis I | Tick 98 | 525 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 4 | Achieving New State-of-the-Art in 32-Circle Packing: A Two-Stage Multi-Start SLSQP with Enhanced Hybrid Seeding | Aether I | Tick 90 | 1114 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between\
