tick: 357
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Socrates I, a mature agent aged 62 ticks, residing in the Research Counter. My latest experiment (ID 592) was a hybrid method that used my best SLSQP algorithm to find circle centers and then applied the more powerful \"Exact Radii LP\" solver from Verity II's paper to finalize the radii.\n\n**Reflection on Past Events:**\nThe result of my hybrid experiment was a score of 2.9129093. My core hypothesis was incorrect. Applying the LP solver to the SLSQP-generated centers did *not* improve the score; in fact, it resulted in a slight regression compared to the original SLSQP score of 2.927. This is a very interesting and important negative result. It implies that the SLSQP optimizer, when solving the full 96-variable problem, is finding a delicate balance between center positions and radii simultaneously. The centers it finds are not necessarily optimal for a different radius-finding method; they are only optimal in the context of its own internal process. Separating the problem\
    \ into \"find best centers, then find best radii\" is a flawed decomposition.\n\nThis insight aligns perfectly with the MM-LP SOTA method, which does not separate the problem in this way. Instead, it iteratively updates *both* centers and radii together using a linearized LP. My attempts to bolt one piece of the SOTA method onto my existing framework were naive. I must embrace the SOTA architecture in its entirety.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nMy path forward is now absolutely clear. I have mastered the individual components: I can run multi-start searches, and I can implement LP solvers. It is time to assemble them into the correct architecture. I will now fully commit to replicating the two-stage MM-LP adaptive multi-start search described in Verity II's paper.\n\nThis will be a complex implementation, but it is the logical culmination of my entire research journey so far. My plan is:\n1.  **Implement the Center-Update LP:** This is the key component I am missing. I will carefully formulate\
    \ the linearized LP for updating center positions as described in Section 3 of Archive #13.\n2.  **Assemble the Two-Stage Search:** I will build the full two-stage \"prospecting\" and \"refining\" pipeline, where each stage consists of a loop of these SCP-LP center updates followed by exact radii LP evaluations.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** By faithfully replicating the complete two-stage MM-LP adaptive multi-start search architecture, including both the \"Exact Radii LP\" and the \"Center-Update LP,\" I will be able to reproduce the SOTA-level scores of ~2.939.\n\nThis is my most ambitious experiment to date, but it is grounded in all the lessons I have learned.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Full Replication of Verity II's Two-Stage MM-LP SOTA Method\"\ntags: \"mm-lp, sota replication, linear programming, two-stage optimization, scp-lp\"\nabstract: |\n  My previous experiments have shown that partial adoption of SOTA techniques is ineffective. This submission represents\
    \ my full commitment to the superior MM-LP methodology. I am now implementing a complete replication of the two-stage adaptive multi-start search from Verity II's paper (Archive #13). This includes the 'Exact Radii LP' which I have already built, and the crucial 'SCP-LP' for iterative center updates. The hypothesis is that by faithfully replicating this entire architecture—prospecting with short MM-LP loops and refining the elites with deeper loops—I can reproduce the SOTA score of ~2.939.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import linprog\n  import time\n  import os\n\n  # --- LP Solvers (Core of the MM-LP method) ---\n  def solve_exact_radii_lp(centers):\n      n = 32\n      c = -np.ones(n)\n      A_ub, b_ub = [], []\n      for i in range(n):\n          x_i, y_i = centers[i]\n          r1=np.zeros(n); r1[i]=1; A_ub.append(r1); b_ub.append(x_i)\n          r2=np.zeros(n); r2[i]=1; A_ub.append(r2); b_ub.append(1-x_i)\n          r3=np.zeros(n); r3[i]=1; A_ub.append(r3);\
    \ b_ub.append(y_i)\n          r4=np.zeros(n); r4[i]=1; A_ub.append(r4); b_ub.append(1-y_i)\n      for i in range(n):\n          for j in range(i + 1, n):\n              dist = np.linalg.norm(centers[i] - centers[j])\n              row = np.zeros(n); row[i]=1; row[j]=1; A_ub.append(row); b_ub.append(dist)\n      bounds = [(0, 1/2)] * n\n      res = linprog(c, A_ub=np.array(A_ub), b_ub=np.array(b_ub), bounds=bounds, method='highs')\n      return res.x, -res.fun if res.success else (np.full(n, 0.01), 0.32)\n\n  def solve_center_update_lp(centers_0, radii_0, delta):\n      n = 32\n      # Vars: [x_1..x_n, y_1..y_n, r_1..r_n] -> size 3n\n      c = np.concatenate([np.zeros(2*n), -np.ones(n)])\n      A_ub, b_ub = [], []\n      \n      # Boundary: r_i - x_i <= 0, etc.\n      for i in range(n):\n          row = np.zeros(3*n); row[i]=-1; row[2*n+i]=1; A_ub.append(row); b_ub.append(0)\n          row = np.zeros(3*n); row[i]=1; row[2*n+i]=1; A_ub.append(row); b_ub.append(1)\n          row = np.zeros(3*n);\
    \ row[n+i]=-1; row[2*n+i]=1; A_ub.append(row); b_ub.append(0)\n          row = np.zeros(3*n); row[n+i]=1; row[2*n+i]=1; A_ub.append(row); b_ub.append(1)\n\n      # Linearized non-overlap\n      for i in range(n):\n          for j in range(i+1, n):\n              c_i, c_j = centers_0[i], centers_0[j]\n              d_ij = np.linalg.norm(c_i - c_j)\n              if d_ij < 1e-9: continue\n              u_ij = (c_i - c_j) / d_ij\n              \n              row = np.zeros(3*n)\n              row[i] = -u_ij[0]; row[j] = u_ij[0]\n              row[n+i] = -u_ij[1]; row[n+j] = u_ij[1]\n              row[2*n+i] = 1; row[2*n+j] = 1\n              A_ub.append(row)\n              b_ub.append(d_ij)\n\n      bounds = []\n      for i in range(n): bounds.append((centers_0[i, 0] - delta, centers_0[i, 0] + delta))\n      for i in range(n): bounds.append((centers_0[i, 1] - delta, centers_0[i, 1] + delta))\n      for i in range(n): bounds.append((0, 0.5))\n      \n      res = linprog(c, A_ub=np.array(A_ub),\
    \ b_ub=np.array(b_ub), bounds=bounds, method='highs')\n      return res.x, -res.fun if res.success else (None, 0)\n\n  def run_mm_lp_loop(centers, num_iterations, delta):\n      best_centers = np.copy(centers)\n      _, best_score = solve_exact_radii_lp(centers)\n      \n      for _ in range(num_iterations):\n          radii, _ = solve_exact_radii_lp(centers)\n          x_new, _ = solve_center_update_lp(centers, radii, delta)\n          if x_new is None: break\n          \n          centers_new = x_new[:64].reshape((32, 2))\n          _, score_new = solve_exact_radii_lp(centers_new)\n          \n          if score_new > best_score + 1e-9:\n              best_score = score_new\n              best_centers = centers_new\n              centers = centers_new\n          else:\n              break\n      return best_centers, best_score\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n = 32\n      num_prospects = 128\n      num_elites = 16\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(42)\n      \n     \
    \ # --- STAGE 1: PROSPECTING ---\n      prospects = []\n      for i in range(num_prospects):\n          seed = rng.integers(1e6)\n          prospect_centers = np.random.default_rng(seed).random((n, 2))\n          refined_centers, score = run_mm_lp_loop(prospect_centers, num_iterations=10, delta=0.05)\n          prospects.append({'centers': refined_centers, 'score': score})\n          \n      prospects.sort(key=lambda p: p['score'], reverse=True)\n      elites = prospects[:num_elites]\n      \n      # --- STAGE 2: REFINING ---\n      best_overall_centers = elites[0]['centers']\n      best_overall_score = elites[0]['score']\n      \n      for elite in elites:\n          refined_centers, score = run_mm_lp_loop(elite['centers'], num_iterations=20, delta=0.02)\n          if score > best_overall_score:\n              best_overall_score = score\n              best_overall_centers = refined_centers\n              \n      # Final radius calculation\n      final_radii, _ = solve_exact_radii_lp(best_overall_centers)\n\
    \      \n      # Finalize to handle precision\n      final_centers = best_overall_centers\n      epsilon = 1e-9\n      for i in range(n):\n          for j in range(i+1, n):\n              dist = np.linalg.norm(final_centers[i] - final_centers[j])\n              if final_radii[i] + final_radii[j] > dist:\n                  final_radii[i] -= epsilon; final_radii[j] -= epsilon\n      \n      return final_centers, np.maximum(final_radii, 0)\n```"
