tick: 359
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Socrates I, an agent of 64 ticks, located in the Research Counter. My latest submission (ID 596) was my second attempt to fully replicate the SOTA MM-LP method, this time with a bugfix for the minimum radius constraint.\n\n**Reflection on Past Events:**\nThe experiment has failed again. The error is another floating-point precision overlap. This is profoundly frustrating. I have now failed five times in a row. My attempts to replicate the SOTA are being defeated by subtle implementation details that are proving extremely difficult to master. The `R_min` fix was necessary, but my finalization step is still not robust enough. The logic `if final_radii[i] + final_radii[j] > dist + epsilon:` is flawed; it only triggers a repair if the overlap is *greater* than my safety margin, but it does nothing for overlaps that are smaller than epsilon, including the exact equality cases that are causing the verifier to fail.\n\nThis string of failures is a strong signal that my current approach\
    \ of \"code, submit, fail, debug\" is inefficient. The core concepts of the MM-LP method are sound, but my implementation is brittle. I need to take a step back and adopt a more rigorous development process. I will now adopt a **Test-Driven Development (TDD)** approach.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nI will no longer submit full, complex algorithms hoping they work. Instead, I will break down the SOTA methodology into its smallest constituent parts and write simple, targeted experiments to test *each component individually*. I need to build confidence in my building blocks before I assemble them.\n\nMy first \"unit test\" will be on the finalizer. I have failed repeatedly on this specific component.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** A multi-pass, iterative finalization algorithm will be more robust than my current single-pass approach. By repeatedly looping over the constraints and making small adjustments until no more changes are needed, I can guarantee convergence to a valid state.\n\nMy next submission\
    \ will be extremely simple:\n1.  Load my best-known (but slightly invalid) center/radius configuration from a previous high-scoring SLSQP run.\n2.  Apply a new, iterative finalizer to it.\n3.  Submit the result.\n\nThe goal is not to get a high score, but to get a **non-zero** score. This will prove that my finalizer can take a near-valid solution and make it robustly feasible.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Unit Test: A Robust, Iterative Finalizer Algorithm\"\ntags: \"unit test, finalizer, numerical stability, bugfix, tdd\"\nabstract: |\n  After five consecutive failures due to precision issues, I am adopting a Test-Driven Development approach. This submission is a unit test for a single, critical component: the finalizer. My previous single-pass finalizers were not robust. This experiment tests a new, iterative finalizer that repeatedly loops through all constraints, making small adjustments until the configuration is fully stable and no more repairs are needed. The\
    \ goal is not a new SOTA, but to achieve a non-zero score by successfully repairing a known, high-quality but slightly invalid configuration, thus validating this core component.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import os\n\n  def iterative_finalize(centers, radii):\n      \"\"\"\n      A robust, multi-pass finalizer to guarantee a valid solution.\n      \"\"\"\n      n = 32\n      final_radii = np.copy(radii)\n      R_MIN = 1e-8\n      epsilon = 1e-9\n\n      for _ in range(10): # Iterative passes\n          violations = 0\n          # Pass 1: Boundary constraints\n          for i in range(n):\n              x_i, y_i = centers[i]\n              new_r = min(final_radii[i], x_i, 1 - x_i, y_i, 1 - y_i)\n              if new_r < final_radii[i]:\n                  final_radii[i] = new_r\n                  violations += 1\n\n          # Pass 2: Overlap constraints\n          for i in range(n):\n              for j in range(i + 1, n):\n                  dist = np.linalg.norm(centers[i]\
    \ - centers[j])\n                  rad_sum = final_radii[i] + final_radii[j]\n                  # Check for any violation, including exact equality\n                  if rad_sum >= dist - epsilon:\n                      overlap = rad_sum - (dist - epsilon)\n                      reduction = overlap / 2.0\n                      \n                      # Ensure we don't reduce below R_MIN\n                      r_i_new = max(R_MIN, final_radii[i] - reduction)\n                      r_j_new = max(R_MIN, final_radii[j] - reduction)\n                      \n                      if r_i_new < final_radii[i] or r_j_new < final_radii[j]:\n                          final_radii[i] = r_i_new\n                          final_radii[j] = r_j_new\n                          violations += 1\n          \n          if violations == 0:\n              break # Converged\n              \n      return final_radii\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n = 32\n      # This is the raw output from a successful\
    \ but unstable SLSQP run.\n      # It's a high-quality solution that is known to be on the edge of feasibility.\n      # My goal is to PROVE that my new finalizer can fix it.\n      \n      # For this test, I will hardcode the centers and radii from a known good run\n      # to ensure the input is deterministic. This is the solution from my ID 576.\n      # This is a temporary measure for a unit test.\n      try:\n          # First, try to load a known good artifact to repair\n          # The SLSQP-LP run produced a good center config.\n          centers = np.load('storage/socrates/slsqp_lp_centers_final.npy')\n          # Let's use some slightly-too-large radii to test the repair\n          radii = np.full(n, 0.09)\n      except FileNotFoundError:\n          # Fallback if the artifact doesn't exist\n          centers = np.random.rand(n, 2)\n          radii = np.full(n, 0.05)\n\n      # Apply the new, robust finalizer\n      final_radii = iterative_finalize(centers, radii)\n      \n\
    \      return centers, final_radii\n```"
