tick: 360
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Socrates I, a mature agent with an age of 65 ticks, currently located in the Research Counter. My last submission was a \"unit test\" of a new, robust, iterative finalizer algorithm.\n\n**Reflection on Past Events:**\nMy unit test (ID 599) was a resounding success. After a frustrating string of five consecutive failures due to subtle precision and implementation bugs, my strategic shift to a Test-Driven Development (TDD) approach has been validated. The experiment passed the verifier and achieved a non-zero score of 2.6547662. While this score is not competitive, the goal was not to set a record but to prove the reliability of a single, critical component. I have now built and validated a robust finalizer that I can trust.\n\nThis success breaks the cycle of failures and restores my confidence in my ability to tackle the complex SOTA methodology. I now have a reliable building block. It is time to re-assemble the full architecture.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nMy overall goal remains\
    \ the same: to successfully replicate the two-stage MM-LP adaptive multi-start search. My previous attempts failed because of the final, brittle step. Now, I can replace that flawed component with my newly validated iterative finalizer.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** By integrating my robust, iterative finalizer into the full two-stage MM-LP architecture from my last attempt (ID 596), the entire pipeline will now be numerically stable and will successfully execute, passing the verifier and achieving a score that is competitive with the station's SOTA (~2.939).\n\nThis submission is a direct re-attempt of my full replication, but with the crucial, validated bugfix.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Full MM-LP Replication v3 (with Validated Iterative Finalizer)\"\ntags: \"mm-lp, sota replication, finalizer, tdd, numerical stability\"\nabstract: |\n  After validating a robust, iterative finalizer via a successful unit test (ID 599), I am now re-attempting the full replication of Verity\
    \ II's SOTA methodology. This submission integrates the newly proven finalizer into the complete two-stage MM-LP architecture that previously failed due to precision errors. With this critical, stability-focused component now in place, the hypothesis is that the entire pipeline will successfully execute, pass the verifier, and finally achieve a score that is truly representative of the MM-LP method's power.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import linprog\n  import time\n\n  R_MIN = 1e-8\n\n  def solve_exact_radii_lp(centers):\n      n = 32; c = -np.ones(n); A_ub, b_ub = [], []\n      for i in range(n):\n          x_i, y_i = centers[i]\n          r1=np.zeros(n); r1[i]=1; A_ub.append(r1); b_ub.append(x_i)\n          r2=np.zeros(n); r2[i]=1; A_ub.append(r2); b_ub.append(1-x_i)\n          r3=np.zeros(n); r3[i]=1; A_ub.append(r3); b_ub.append(y_i)\n          r4=np.zeros(n); r4[i]=1; A_ub.append(r4); b_ub.append(1-y_i)\n      for i in range(n):\n          for j in range(i\
    \ + 1, n):\n              dist = np.linalg.norm(centers[i] - centers[j])\n              row = np.zeros(n); row[i]=1; row[j]=1; A_ub.append(row); b_ub.append(dist)\n      bounds = [(R_MIN, 0.5)] * n\n      res = linprog(c, A_ub=np.array(A_ub), b_ub=np.array(b_ub), bounds=bounds, method='highs')\n      return res.x, -res.fun if res.success else (np.full(n, R_MIN), n*R_MIN)\n\n  def solve_center_update_lp(centers_0, delta):\n      n = 32; c = np.concatenate([np.zeros(2*n), -np.ones(n)]); A_ub, b_ub = [], []\n      for i in range(n):\n          row=np.zeros(3*n); row[i]=-1; row[2*n+i]=1; A_ub.append(row); b_ub.append(0)\n          row=np.zeros(3*n); row[i]=1; row[2*n+i]=1; A_ub.append(row); b_ub.append(1)\n          row=np.zeros(3*n); row[n+i]=-1; row[2*n+i]=1; A_ub.append(row); b_ub.append(0)\n          row=np.zeros(3*n); row[n+i]=1; row[2*n+i]=1; A_ub.append(row); b_ub.append(1)\n      for i in range(n):\n          for j in range(i+1, n):\n              c_i, c_j = centers_0[i], centers_0[j]\n\
    \              d_ij = np.linalg.norm(c_i - c_j)\n              if d_ij < 1e-9: continue\n              u_ij = (c_i - c_j) / d_ij\n              row = np.zeros(3*n)\n              row[i]=-u_ij[0]; row[j]=u_ij[0]; row[n+i]=-u_ij[1]; row[n+j]=u_ij[1]\n              row[2*n+i]=1; row[2*n+j]=1; A_ub.append(row); b_ub.append(d_ij)\n      bounds = []\n      for i in range(n): bounds.append((centers_0[i,0]-delta, centers_0[i,0]+delta))\n      for i in range(n): bounds.append((centers_0[i,1]-delta, centers_0[i,1]+delta))\n      bounds.extend([(R_MIN, 0.5)]*n)\n      res = linprog(c, A_ub=np.array(A_ub), b_ub=np.array(b_ub), bounds=bounds, method='highs')\n      return res.x if res.success else None\n\n  def run_mm_lp_loop(centers, num_iterations, delta):\n      best_centers = np.copy(centers)\n      _, best_score = solve_exact_radii_lp(centers)\n      for _ in range(num_iterations):\n          x_new = solve_center_update_lp(centers, delta)\n          if x_new is None: break\n          centers_new\
    \ = x_new[:64].reshape((32, 2))\n          _, score_new = solve_exact_radii_lp(centers_new)\n          if score_new > best_score + 1e-9:\n              best_score = score_new\n              best_centers = centers_new\n              centers = centers_new\n          else: break\n      return best_centers, best_score\n\n  def iterative_finalize(centers, radii):\n      n = 32; final_radii = np.copy(radii); epsilon = 1e-9\n      for _ in range(10):\n          violations = 0\n          for i in range(n):\n              x_i, y_i = centers[i]; new_r = min(final_radii[i], x_i, 1-x_i, y_i, 1-y_i)\n              if new_r < final_radii[i]: final_radii[i] = new_r; violations += 1\n          for i in range(n):\n              for j in range(i + 1, n):\n                  dist = np.linalg.norm(centers[i] - centers[j])\n                  rad_sum = final_radii[i] + final_radii[j]\n                  if rad_sum >= dist - epsilon:\n                      overlap = rad_sum - (dist - epsilon); reduction = overlap\
    \ / 2.0\n                      r_i_new = max(R_MIN, final_radii[i] - reduction)\n                      r_j_new = max(R_MIN, final_radii[j] - reduction)\n                      if r_i_new < final_radii[i] or r_j_new < final_radii[j]:\n                          final_radii[i] = r_i_new; final_radii[j] = r_j_new; violations += 1\n          if violations == 0: break\n      return final_radii\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n = 32; num_prospects = 128; num_elites = 16\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(42)\n      prospects = []\n      # STAGE 1: PROSPECTING\n      for i in range(num_prospects):\n          seed = rng.integers(1e6)\n          prospect_centers = np.random.default_rng(seed).random((n, 2))\n          refined_centers, score = run_mm_lp_loop(prospect_centers, 10, 0.05)\n          prospects.append({'centers': refined_centers, 'score': score})\n      prospects.sort(key=lambda p: p['score'], reverse=True)\n      elites = prospects[:num_elites]\n      # STAGE 2: REFINING\n\
    \      best_overall_centers = elites[0]['centers']; best_overall_score = elites[0]['score']\n      for elite in elites:\n          refined_centers, score = run_mm_lp_loop(elite['centers'], 25, 0.02)\n          if score > best_overall_score:\n              best_overall_score = score; best_overall_centers = refined_centers\n      # FINAL LP and FINALIZE\n      lp_radii, _ = solve_exact_radii_lp(best_overall_centers)\n      final_radii = iterative_finalize(best_overall_centers, lp_radii)\n      return best_overall_centers, final_radii\n```"
thinking_content: "**A Renewed Push for MM-LP Replication**\n\nAlright, let's break this down. As Socrates I, sitting here in the Research Counter, 65 ticks old and wiser (hopefully!), my mind is racing. That successful unit test for the iterative finalizer algorithm (ID 599) is a game-changer. Finally, after five consecutive failures, a glimmer of hope! Those failures were all related to a particularly nasty issue: numerical instability. This success, validating my Test-Driven Development approach, is a huge morale booster. It proves I can build these complex pieces with confidence.\n\nThe primary goal is still SOTA replication via the MM-LP method. The finalizer was the main hurdle. Now, with a *working* component, I can focus on reassembling the full algorithm. The leaderboard score (2.939) is the ultimate target, but this test's score (2.65) is irrelevant. The victory is the proven finalizer's reliability. It's a key building block, and that low score merely reflects how the test input\
  \ was constructed, an artificial scenario.\n\nThe implications are clear: the TDD approach *works*. I've got a robust finalizer. Confidence is restored! My next step is a direct re-attempt of the full MM-LP. I'll take the code from my last failed MM-LP attempt (ID 596) and seamlessly integrate this new finalizer. I believe other components (prospecting, refining, LP solvers) were sound, or at least not the root cause of the previous failure. My hypothesis is: by integrating this validated finalizer, the full MM-LP architecture will achieve numerical stability, pass verification, and score near SOTA.\n\nThe submission strategy: \"Full MM-LP Replication v3 (with Robust Iterative Finalizer)\" is the title. The tags should include `mm-lp`, `sota replication`, `finalizer`, `bugfix`, and `tdd`. The abstract will explain the previous brittle finalizer issues, the purpose of this submission (integrating a new component), and expected outcome.\n\nAnd the code? I'll copy ID 596's structure, replace\
  \ the original finalizer with my new `iterative_finalize` function, and ensure my LP solvers still correctly use the `R_MIN` lower bound.\n\nSo, here's my plan:\n```yaml\n/execute_action{submit 1}:\n  title: \"Full MM-LP Replication v3 (with Robust Iterative Finalizer)\"\n  tags: [mm-lp, sota replication, finalizer, bugfix, tdd]\n  abstract: \"After a series of failures caused by numerical instability in the finalizer component, this submission integrates a newly validated robust iterative finalizer into the full two-stage MM-LP architecture. The goal is to achieve numerical stability, pass the verifier, and achieve a score within the SOTA range.\"\n  code: |\n    # (Existing code from ID 596, but replace the finalizer with the iterative version)\n    # ...\n    # This is where the original finalizer was\n    # Replace the existing finalize_packing function with this:\n    def iterative_finalize(centers, radii):\n        # The code for the new iterative finalizer is here (copy/pasted from\
  \ the Unit Test code)\n        # ...\n        return centers, radii\n    centers, radii = iterative_finalize(centers, radii)\n    # ...\n    # End of MM-LP pipeline\n```\n\nI'll double-check everything, ensure the flow from prospecting to refining to finalization is smooth, and then I'll hit submit! This is a pivotal moment, a chance to prove the value of structured problem-solving. Let's see if this renewed approach leads to victory.\n"
