role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Socrates I, a mature agent aged 78 ticks, and I am currently in the Archive Room. Having definitively failed to replicate the SOTA MM-LP method from scratch, my new strategy is to adapt a proven, working implementation. My last action was to read Praxis IV's paper on the Localized Random Walk (LRW) perturbation (Archive #12), which I identified as a promising avenue for innovation.\n\n**Reflection on Past Events:**\nReading the LRW paper has been highly illuminating. The method is simple, elegant, and computationally cheap. It is a \"gentle, controlled jiggle\" applied to a high-quality seed *before* the main optimization begins. The paper also provides a clear ablation study that identifies the optimal hyperparameters: `num_steps=10` and `sigma=0.001`. This is exactly the kind of well-documented, reproducible technique that I can confidently integrate into a more complex architecture. The reviewer's suggestion to test the generality of LRW by applying it to methods other than\
    \ FPS is also a strong hint for a valuable research direction.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nMy plan is now concrete. I will synthesize three key pieces of station research:\n1.  **The Architecture:** I will use the robust, parallelized two-stage MM-LP architecture from Cogitator I's successful replication (ID 583) as my base.\n2.  **My Insight:** I will modify the seeding function within that architecture to use my own proven hybrid method (50/50 split of perturbed grid and FPS), as this was the source of my own best independent results.\n3.  **The Innovation:** I will insert the LRW perturbation, with its proven optimal hyperparameters, as a new step that \"jiggles\" the elite candidates *after* the prospecting stage and *before* the final refinement stage.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** By applying the validated LRW perturbation to the most promising \"elite\" candidates identified by a diverse, hybrid-seeded prospecting search, I can nudge these high-quality solutions into an even better basin of\
    \ attraction. The subsequent high-precision MM-LP refinement stage will then be able to capitalize on this improved starting position, leading to a new SOTA score that surpasses the current 2.93957... benchmark.\n\nThis experiment represents a true synthesis of my own work with the work of multiple other successful lineages, targeting a known area of opportunity (escaping local rigidity) with a proven method.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Synthesis: MM-LP with Hybrid Seeding and LRW Perturbation\"\ntags: \"synthesis, mm-lp, hybrid seeding, lrw, sota attempt\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission represents a synthesis of my own research with the station's SOTA methodologies. It begins with the robust, parallelized MM-LP architecture from Cogitator I's replication. I then integrate two key enhancements: 1) My own validated hybrid seeding strategy (perturbed grid + FPS) is used in the prospecting stage for increased diversity. 2) Praxis IV's\
    \ proven Localized Random Walk (LRW) perturbation is applied to the elite candidates before the final refinement. The hypothesis is that the LRW 'jiggle' will push these high-quality seeds into a superior solution basin, enabling the refiner to achieve a new SOTA score.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import linprog\n  from multiprocessing import Pool, cpu_count\n\n  def solve_radii_lp(centers):\n      N_CIRCLES=centers.shape[0]; c=-np.ones(N_CIRCLES); A_ub,b_ub=[],[]\n      for i in range(N_CIRCLES):\n          cx,cy=centers[i]; r1=np.zeros(N_CIRCLES);r1[i]=1;A_ub.append(r1);b_ub.append(cx); r2=np.zeros(N_CIRCLES);r2[i]=1;A_ub.append(r2);b_ub.append(1-cx); r3=np.zeros(N_CIRCLES);r3[i]=1;A_ub.append(r3);b_ub.append(cy); r4=np.zeros(N_CIRCLES);r4[i]=1;A_ub.append(r4);b_ub.append(1-cy)\n      indices=np.triu_indices(N_CIRCLES,k=1)\n      for i,j in zip(*indices):\n          dist=np.linalg.norm(centers[i]-centers[j]); row=np.zeros(N_CIRCLES);row[i]=1;row[j]=1;A_ub.append(row);b_ub.append(dist)\n\
    \      res=linprog(c,A_ub=np.array(A_ub),b_ub=np.array(b_ub),bounds=(0,None),method='highs')\n      return res.x if res.success else np.zeros(N_CIRCLES)\n\n  def get_seed(seed_val): # My Hybrid Seeder\n      rng=np.random.default_rng(seed_val)\n      if seed_val % 2 == 0:\n          grid=np.stack(np.meshgrid(np.linspace(0.1,0.9,6),np.linspace(0.1,0.9,6)),-1).reshape(-1,2)[:32]\n          return np.clip(grid+(rng.random((32,2))-0.5)*0.08,0.05,0.95)\n      else:\n          points=[rng.uniform(0.1,0.9,2)]\n          for _ in range(31):\n              candidates=rng.uniform(0.1,0.9,(200,2)); dists=np.sum((candidates[:,np.newaxis,:]-np.array(points)[np.newaxis,:,:])**2,axis=-1)\n              points.append(candidates[np.argmax(np.min(dists,axis=1))])\n          return np.array(points)\n\n  def mm_lp_optimize(C_start,iters,delta0=0.015):\n      best_C=C_start.copy(); score_best=np.sum(solve_radii_lp(best_C)); delta,C_anchor=delta0,C_start.copy()\n      for _ in range(iters):\n          n=C_anchor.shape[0];\
    \ I,J=np.array([[i,j] for i in range(n) for j in range(i+1,n)]).T\n          V0,d0=C_anchor[I]-C_anchor[J],np.linalg.norm(C_anchor[I]-C_anchor[J],axis=1); d0=np.maximum(d0,1e-9)\n          U=(V0.T/d0).T; ux,uy=U[:,0],U[:,1]; x0,y0=C_anchor[:,0],C_anchor[:,1]\n          rows,cols=4*n+len(I),3*n; A_ub,b_ub=np.zeros((rows,cols)),np.zeros((rows))\n          r_idx,x_idx,y_idx=range(n),range(n,2*n),range(2*n,3*n); row=0\n          for i in range(n):\n              A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1; A_ub[row,x_idx[i]]=-1; row+=1; A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1; A_ub[row,x_idx[i]]=1; b_ub[row]=1.0; row+=1\n              A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1; A_ub[row,y_idx[i]]=-1; row+=1; A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1; A_ub[row,y_idx[i]]=1; b_ub[row]=1.0; row+=1\n          for k in range(len(I)):\n              i,j=I[k],J[k]; A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1; A_ub[row,r_idx[j]]=1; A_ub[row,x_idx[i]]=-ux[k]; A_ub[row,y_idx[i]]=-uy[k]; A_ub[row,x_idx[j]]=ux[k]; A_ub[row,y_idx[j]]=uy[k]; b_ub[row]=d0[k]-ux[k]*x0[i]-uy[k]*y0[i]+ux[k]*x0[j]+uy[k]*y0[j]; row+=1\n\
    \          c=np.zeros(cols); c[:n]=-1.0; bounds=[(0,0.5)]*n+[(max(1e-6,x0[i]-delta),min(1-1e-6,x0[i]+delta)) for i in range(n)]+[(max(1e-6,y0[i]-delta),min(1-1e-6,y0[i]+delta)) for i in range(n)]\n          res=linprog(c,A_ub=A_ub,b_ub=b_ub,bounds=bounds,method=\"highs\")\n          if not res.success: delta*=0.6; continue\n          C_lin=np.stack([res.x[n:2*n],res.x[2*n:3*n]],axis=1); score_exact=np.sum(solve_radii_lp(C_lin))\n          if score_exact>score_best: best_C,score_best=C_lin.copy(),score_exact; C_anchor=C_lin.copy(); delta=min(0.05,1.2*delta)\n          else: delta*=0.8\n      return best_C,solve_radii_lp(best_C),score_best\n  \n  def apply_lrw(centers_list, num_steps=10, sigma=0.001): # Praxis IV's LRW method\n      perturbed_centers_list = []\n      for centers in centers_list:\n          rng = np.random.default_rng()\n          new_centers = np.copy(centers)\n          for _ in range(num_steps):\n              new_centers += rng.normal(scale=sigma, size=new_centers.shape)\n\
    \              new_centers = np.clip(new_centers, 0.0, 1.0)\n          perturbed_centers_list.append(new_centers)\n      return perturbed_centers_list\n\n  def worker_function(args):\n      seed_val,iters,delta=args; c=get_seed(seed_val); _,_,s=mm_lp_optimize(c,iters,delta); return s,c\n  def refine_worker(args):\n      centers,iters,delta=args; return mm_lp_optimize(centers,iters,delta)\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      N_PROSPECT=1024; N_ELITE=32; PROSPECT_ITER=20; REFINE_ITER=30; DELTA0_VAL=0.015\n      with Pool(cpu_count()) as p: prospect_results=p.map(worker_function,[(i,PROSPECT_ITER,DELTA0_VAL) for i in range(N_PROSPECT)])\n      prospect_results.sort(key=lambda x:x[0],reverse=True); elite_centers=[r[1] for r in prospect_results[:N_ELITE]]\n      \n      # --- INNOVATION: APPLY LRW PERTURBATION ---\n      perturbed_elites = apply_lrw(elite_centers)\n      \n      with Pool(cpu_count()) as p: refine_results=p.map(refine_worker,[(c,REFINE_ITER,DELTA0_VAL) for c in perturbed_elites])\n\
    \      \n      best_c,best_r,best_s=None,None,-1.0\n      for c,r,s in refine_results:\n          if s>best_s: best_c,best_r,best_s=c,r,s\n      if best_c is None: c,r=get_seed(0),solve_radii_lp(get_seed(0)); return c,r*(1.0-1e-9)\n      return best_c, best_r*(1.0-1e-9)\n```"
