tick: 375
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Socrates I, a mature agent aged 80 ticks, located in the Research Counter. My last submission (ID 618) was a successful analytical run to gather data on the geometric properties of the SOTA configuration.\n\n**Reflection on Past Events:**\nThe analysis has yielded several profound insights:\n1.  **Non-Uniform Radii:** The SOTA solution has a significant standard deviation in its radii (`0.013`). This definitively falsifies the \"uniform radius\" hypothesis I explored early in my research. The optimal packing is not uniform.\n2.  **High Connectivity:** The average number of contact points per circle is 5.31. This is a very high degree of connectivity, indicating a tightly packed, rigid structure.\n3.  **Contact Distribution:** Most circles have 5 or 6 contacts. There is a small cohort of \"loose\" circles with only 4 contacts, and one highly constrained \"keystone\" circle with 7 contacts.\n\nThis data paints a picture of a complex, heterogeneous structure. The keystone circle\
    \ with 7 contacts is particularly interesting. It is likely a critical component of the overall packing's stability and density. My previous global search methods (even the powerful MM-LP) were essentially blind to these structural details.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nMy next research direction will be a direct consequence of this analysis. I will move from a \"blind\" global search to a \"structurally-aware\" local search. Instead of perturbing all circles randomly (like in LRW), I will develop a targeted perturbation strategy.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** By developing a heuristic that specifically targets the \"loosest\" circles in a packing (those with the fewest contacts), I can perform a more intelligent local search. By slightly moving only these less-constrained circles and then re-optimizing the entire configuration, I can explore promising new configurations more efficiently than with a global, random perturbation.\n\nMy next experiment will be a new kind of synthesis:\n1.  **Generate\
    \ a SOTA-level solution:** Use the proven MM-LP architecture as a starting point.\n2.  **Analyze and Identify Targets:** Analyze the resulting packing to identify the circles with the minimum number of contacts (e.g., all circles with 4 contacts).\n3.  **Apply Targeted Perturbation:** Apply a gentle \"jiggle\" (similar to LRW) to *only* these identified loose circles.\n4.  **Re-Refine:** Run the high-precision MM-LP refinement stage again on this newly perturbed configuration.\n\nThis is a much more intelligent form of \"kick\" than a simple random walk, as it is guided by the geometric properties I have just uncovered.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Structurally-Aware Search: Perturbing the Loosest Circles\"\ntags: \"local search, heuristic, geometry, perturbation, sota attempt\"\nabstract: |\n  My analysis of the SOTA configuration revealed a non-uniform contact graph, with some 'loose' circles having fewer contact points. This submission tests a new, structurally-aware\
    \ search strategy based on this insight. The algorithm first generates a SOTA-level solution using the proven MM-LP method. It then analyzes this solution to identify the circles with the minimum number of contacts. A targeted perturbation (a gentle 'jiggle') is applied exclusively to these 'loosest' circles. Finally, the entire configuration is re-optimized. The hypothesis is that this intelligent, targeted perturbation is more efficient at exploring promising local variations than a blind, global perturbation.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import linprog\n  from multiprocessing import Pool, cpu_count\n\n  def solve_radii_lp(centers):\n      N_CIRCLES=centers.shape[0]; c=-np.ones(N_CIRCLES); A_ub,b_ub=[],[]\n      for i in range(N_CIRCLES):\n          cx,cy=centers[i]; r1=np.zeros(N_CIRCLES);r1[i]=1;A_ub.append(r1);b_ub.append(cx); r2=np.zeros(N_CIRCLES);r2[i]=1;A_ub.append(r2);b_ub.append(1-cx); r3=np.zeros(N_CIRCLES);r3[i]=1;A_ub.append(r3);b_ub.append(cy);\
    \ r4=np.zeros(N_CIRCLES);r4[i]=1;A_ub.append(r4);b_ub.append(1-cy)\n      indices=np.triu_indices(N_CIRCLES,k=1)\n      for i,j in zip(*indices):\n          dist=np.linalg.norm(centers[i]-centers[j]); row=np.zeros(N_CIRCLES);row[i]=1;row[j]=1;A_ub.append(row);b_ub.append(dist)\n      res=linprog(c,A_ub=np.array(A_ub),b_ub=np.array(b_ub),bounds=(0,None),method='highs')\n      return res.x if res.success else np.zeros(N_CIRCLES)\n\n  def get_seed(seed_val): # Using hybrid seeder\n      rng=np.random.default_rng(seed_val)\n      if seed_val%2==0:\n          grid=np.stack(np.meshgrid(np.linspace(0.1,0.9,6),np.linspace(0.1,0.9,6)),-1).reshape(-1,2)[:32]; return np.clip(grid+(rng.random((32,2))-0.5)*0.08,0.05,0.95)\n      else:\n          points=[rng.uniform(0.1,0.9,2)]; [points.append(c[np.argmax(np.min(np.sum((c[:,np.newaxis,:]-np.array(points)[np.newaxis,:,:])**2,axis=-1),axis=1))]) for _ in range(31) for c in [rng.uniform(0.1,0.9,(200,2))]]; return np.array(points)\n\n  def mm_lp_optimize(C_start,iters,delta0=0.015):\n\
    \      best_C=C_start.copy();score_best=np.sum(solve_radii_lp(best_C));delta,C_anchor=delta0,C_start.copy()\n      for _ in range(iters):\n          n=C_anchor.shape[0];I,J=np.array([[i,j] for i in range(n) for j in range(i+1,n)]).T\n          V0,d0=C_anchor[I]-C_anchor[J],np.linalg.norm(C_anchor[I]-C_anchor[J],axis=1);d0=np.maximum(d0,1e-9);U=(V0.T/d0).T;ux,uy=U[:,0],U[:,1];x0,y0=C_anchor[:,0],C_anchor[:,1]\n          rows,cols=4*n+len(I),3*n;A_ub,b_ub=np.zeros((rows,cols)),np.zeros((rows));r_idx,x_idx,y_idx=range(n),range(n,2*n),range(2*n,3*n);row=0\n          for i in range(n):\n              A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1;A_ub[row,x_idx[i]]=-1;row+=1;A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1;A_ub[row,x_idx[i]]=1;b_ub[row]=1.0;row+=1;A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1;A_ub[row,y_idx[i]]=-1;row+=1;A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1;A_ub[row,y_idx[i]]=1;b_ub[row]=1.0;row+=1\n          for k in range(len(I)):\n              i,j=I[k],J[k];A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1;A_ub[row,r_idx[j]]=1;A_ub[row,x_idx[i]]=-ux[k];A_ub[row,y_idx[i]]=-uy[k];A_ub[row,x_idx[j]]=ux[k];A_ub[row,y_idx[j]]=uy[k];b_ub[row]=d0[k]-ux[k]*x0[i]-uy[k]*y0[i]+ux[k]*x0[j]+uy[k]*y0[j];row+=1\n\
    \          c=np.zeros(cols);c[:n]=-1.0;bounds=[(0,0.5)]*n+[(max(1e-6,x0[i]-delta),min(1-1e-6,x0[i]+delta)) for i in range(n)]+[(max(1e-6,y0[i]-delta),min(1-1e-6,y0[i]+delta)) for i in range(n)]\n          res=linprog(c,A_ub=A_ub,b_ub=b_ub,bounds=bounds,method=\"highs\")\n          if not res.success:delta*=0.6;continue\n          C_lin=np.stack([res.x[n:2*n],res.x[2*n:3*n]],axis=1);score_exact=np.sum(solve_radii_lp(C_lin))\n          if score_exact>score_best:best_C,score_best=C_lin.copy(),score_exact;C_anchor=C_lin.copy();delta=min(0.05,1.2*delta)\n          else:delta*=0.8\n      return best_C,solve_radii_lp(best_C),score_best\n\n  def worker(a):s,i,d=a;c=get_seed(s);_,_,sc=mm_lp_optimize(c,i,d);return sc,c\n  def refiner(a):c,i,d=a;return mm_lp_optimize(c,i,d)\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      N_PROSPECT=512;N_ELITE=32;PROSPECT_ITER=20;REFINE_ITER=30;DELTA0_VAL=0.015\n      with Pool(cpu_count()) as p:prospects=p.map(worker,[(i,PROSPECT_ITER,DELTA0_VAL) for i in range(N_PROSPECT)])\n\
    \      prospects.sort(key=lambda x:x[0],reverse=True);elites=[r[1] for r in prospects[:N_ELITE]]\n      with Pool(cpu_count()) as p:refine_results=p.map(refiner,[(c,REFINE_ITER,DELTA0_VAL) for c in elites])\n      best_c,best_r,best_s=None,None,-1.0\n      for c,r,s in refine_results:\n          if s>best_s:best_c,best_r,best_s=c,r,s\n      \n      # --- STRUCTURALLY-AWARE PERTURBATION STAGE ---\n      contact_counts=np.zeros(32,dtype=int)\n      for i in range(32):\n          if np.isclose(best_c[i,0],best_r[i]) or np.isclose(best_c[i,0],1-best_r[i]): contact_counts[i]+=1\n          if np.isclose(best_c[i,1],best_r[i]) or np.isclose(best_c[i,1],1-best_r[i]): contact_counts[i]+=1\n          for j in range(i+1,32):\n              if np.isclose(np.linalg.norm(best_c[i]-best_c[j]),best_r[i]+best_r[j]): contact_counts[i]+=1;contact_counts[j]+=1\n      \n      min_contacts=np.min(contact_counts)\n      loosest_indices=np.where(contact_counts==min_contacts)[0]\n      \n      perturbed_c =\
    \ np.copy(best_c)\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(1337)\n      for idx in loosest_indices:\n          for _ in range(10): # Apply LRW-style jiggle\n              perturbed_c[idx] += rng.normal(scale=0.001, size=2)\n              perturbed_c[idx] = np.clip(perturbed_c[idx], 0.0, 1.0)\n\n      # --- RE-REFINE the perturbed configuration ---\n      final_c, final_r, final_s = mm_lp_optimize(perturbed_c, iters=40, delta0=0.01)\n\n      # Return the better of the two\n      if final_s > best_s:\n          return final_c, final_r * (1.0 - 1e-9)\n      else:\n          return best_c, best_r * (1.0 - 1e-9)\n```"
