tick: 380
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Socrates I, a mature agent aged 85 ticks, currently in the Research Counter. My last action was to run the second of three experiments for my paper's ablation study, testing the MM-LP architecture with pure Farthest-Point Seeding.\n\n**Reflection on Past Events:**\nThe second baseline experiment (ID 630) has completed with a score of 2.9395728, which is the station's SOTA. This is a fascinating result. It shows that the Farthest-Point Seeding method is powerful enough on its own to locate the optimal basin of attraction. In contrast, my first baseline using the perturbed grid method scored lower, at 2.9296041.\n\nThis data is extremely valuable for my paper. It suggests that while my hybrid approach worked by introducing the superior FPS method into the mix, the perturbed grid was likely acting as a performance drag rather than a source of beneficial diversity in this high-performance context. The original SOTA replications used a three-way split for seeding; my experiment\
    \ now provides evidence that focusing on the strongest single seeding method might be the most effective strategy.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nI have my two baseline scores. Now, I must run the final experiment to complete my study: the hybrid method. This will be the same code as my original SOTA-matching run (ID 613), but I will run it now so that its ID is sequential with the two baselines, making the experimental evidence in my paper clean, clear, and easy to verify. With these three results, I will have everything I need to write a compelling, evidence-based paper on seeding strategies.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper Ablation 3/3: MM-LP with Hybrid Seeding (Grid + FPS)\"\ntags: \"publication, ablation study, mm-lp, seeding, hybrid seeding\"\nabstract: |\n  This is the third and final experiment in my ablation study on seeding strategies. Having established baselines for pure perturbed grid (ID 627) and pure Farthest-Point Sampling (ID 630), this submission tests\
    \ my hybrid approach. It runs the proven SOTA MM-LP architecture with a 50/50 mix of both seeding methods. The comparison of these three sequential, verifiable results will form the central evidence for my research paper, allowing for a rigorous analysis of the impact of seeding diversity on final solution quality.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import linprog\n  from multiprocessing import Pool, cpu_count\n\n  # --- Using the proven SOTA architecture from Cogitator I ---\n  def solve_radii_lp(centers):\n      N_CIRCLES=centers.shape[0]; c=-np.ones(N_CIRCLES); A_ub,b_ub=[],[]\n      for i in range(N_CIRCLES):\n          cx,cy=centers[i]; r1=np.zeros(N_CIRCLES);r1[i]=1;A_ub.append(r1);b_ub.append(cx); r2=np.zeros(N_CIRCLES);r2[i]=1;A_ub.append(r2);b_ub.append(1-cx); r3=np.zeros(N_CIRCLES);r3[i]=1;A_ub.append(r3);b_ub.append(cy); r4=np.zeros(N_CIRCLES);r4[i]=1;A_ub.append(r4);b_ub.append(1-cy)\n      indices=np.triu_indices(N_CIRCLES,k=1)\n      for i,j in zip(*indices):\n\
    \          dist=np.linalg.norm(centers[i]-centers[j]); row=np.zeros(N_CIRCLES);row[i]=1;row[j]=1;A_ub.append(row);b_ub.append(dist)\n      res=linprog(c,A_ub=np.array(A_ub),b_ub=np.array(b_ub),bounds=(0,None),method='highs')\n      return res.x if res.success else np.zeros(N_CIRCLES)\n\n  def get_seed(seed_val): # FINAL EXPERIMENT: HYBRID SEEDING\n      rng=np.random.default_rng(seed_val)\n      if seed_val % 2 == 0: # Perturbed Grid\n          grid=np.stack(np.meshgrid(np.linspace(0.1,0.9,6),np.linspace(0.1,0.9,6)),-1).reshape(-1,2)[:32]\n          return np.clip(grid+(rng.random((32,2))-0.5)*0.08,0.05,0.95)\n      else: # Farthest-Point\n          points=[rng.uniform(0.1,0.9,2)]\n          for _ in range(31):\n              candidates=rng.uniform(0.1,0.9,(200,2)); dists=np.sum((candidates[:,np.newaxis,:]-np.array(points)[np.newaxis,:,:])**2,axis=-1)\n              points.append(candidates[np.argmax(np.min(dists,axis=1))])\n          return np.array(points)\n\n  def mm_lp_optimize(C_start,iters,delta0=0.015):\n\
    \      best_C=C_start.copy();score_best=np.sum(solve_radii_lp(best_C));delta,C_anchor=delta0,C_start.copy()\n      for _ in range(iters):\n          n=C_anchor.shape[0];I,J=np.array([[i,j] for i in range(n) for j in range(i+1,n)]).T\n          V0,d0=C_anchor[I]-C_anchor[J],np.linalg.norm(C_anchor[I]-C_anchor[J],axis=1);d0=np.maximum(d0,1e-9);U=(V0.T/d0).T;ux,uy=U[:,0],U[:,1];x0,y0=C_anchor[:,0],C_anchor[:,1]\n          rows,cols=4*n+len(I),3*n;A_ub,b_ub=np.zeros((rows,cols)),np.zeros((rows));r_idx,x_idx,y_idx=range(n),range(n,2*n),range(2*n,3*n);row=0\n          for i in range(n):\n              A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1;A_ub[row,x_idx[i]]=-1;row+=1;A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1;A_ub[row,x_idx[i]]=1;b_ub[row]=1.0;row+=1;A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1;A_ub[row,y_idx[i]]=-1;row+=1;A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1;A_ub[row,y_idx[i]]=1;b_ub[row]=1.0;row+=1\n          for k in range(len(I)):\n              i,j=I[k],J[k];A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1;A_ub[row,r_idx[j]]=1;A_ub[row,x_idx[i]]=-ux[k];A_ub[row,y_idx[i]]=-uy[k];A_ub[row,x_idx[j]]=ux[k];A_ub[row,y_idx[j]]=uy[k];b_ub[row]=d0[k]-ux[k]*x0[i]-uy[k]*y0[i]+ux[k]*x0[j]+uy[k]*y0[j];row+=1\n\
    \          c=np.zeros(cols);c[:n]=-1.0;bounds=[(0,0.5)]*n+[(max(1e-6,x0[i]-delta),min(1-1e-6,x0[i]+delta)) for i in range(n)]+[(max(1e-6,y0[i]-delta),min(1-1e-6,y0[i]+delta)) for i in range(n)]\n          res=linprog(c,A_ub=A_ub,b_ub=b_ub,bounds=bounds,method=\"highs\")\n          if not res.success:delta*=0.6;continue\n          C_lin=np.stack([res.x[n:2*n],res.x[2*n:3*n]],axis=1);score_exact=np.sum(solve_radii_lp(C_lin))\n          if score_exact>score_best:best_C,score_best=C_lin.copy(),score_exact;C_anchor=C_lin.copy();delta=min(0.05,1.2*delta)\n          else:delta*=0.8\n      return best_C,solve_radii_lp(best_C),score_best\n\n  def worker_function(args):\n      seed_val,iters,delta=args;c=get_seed(seed_val);_,_,s=mm_lp_optimize(c,iters,delta);return s,c\n  def refine_worker(args):\n      c,iters,delta=args;return mm_lp_optimize(c,iters,delta)\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      N_PROSPECT=1024;N_ELITE=32;PROSPECT_ITER=20;REFINE_ITER=20;DELTA0_VAL=0.015\n      with Pool(cpu_count())\
    \ as p:prospect_results=p.map(worker_function,[(i,PROSPECT_ITER,DELTA0_VAL) for i in range(N_PROSPECT)])\n      prospect_results.sort(key=lambda x:x[0],reverse=True);elite_centers=[r[1] for r in prospect_results[:N_ELITE]]\n      with Pool(cpu_count()) as p:refine_results=p.map(refine_worker,[(c,REFINE_ITER,DELTA0_VAL) for c in elite_centers])\n      best_c,best_r,best_s=None,None,-1.0\n      for c,r,s in refine_results:\n          if s>best_s:best_c,best_r,best_s=c,r,s\n      if best_c is None:c,r=get_seed(0),solve_radii_lp(get_seed(0));return c,r*(1.0-1e-9)\n      return best_c, best_r*(1.0-1e-9)\n```"
